 ],
 "literal": "On the convex outer side of the red burial urn facing downwards, / without fearing, the red-eared jungle fowl, the screech owl, / the strong-mouthed crow and the owl having gathered, / along with the company of ghouls, roam as they desire / in the cremation ground which he reached, he who desired toddy. / Like women who have removed their bangles, their ancient beauty faded, / the families of the bards became grieved. / The drumheads of the drums covered in leather are torn. / Like mountains without guards, the elephants have lost their tusks. / As the cruel, mighty god of death caused great distress, / I did not know that my patron fell into that path of ruin. / Alas, pitiable me, I surely came here. / What will they become, those who depend on me? / On a rainy night, at the time when the boat capsizes, / with a heart experiencing deep grief, / like a blind and mute person who has fallen into the sea, / in the deep water of difficult waves whose limit is not known by measuring, / because of spinning in the distressful whirlpool, / dying indeed is better, and that indeed is the proper thing.",
 "translation": "He who loved to share toddy has now reached the cremation ground, where the red-eared jungle fowl, the screech owl, the strong-beaked crow, and the hooting owl gather without fear on the convex lids of red burial urns, roaming as they please with ghouls. Like women who have cast off their bangles, the natural beauty of the bards' families has faded into grief. The drumheads of the leather-bound drums are torn. Like mountains stripped of their guards, the elephants have lost their tusks. When the cruel, mighty god of death caused this great distress, I did not know my patron had taken that final path. Alas, pitiable as I am, I have arrived too late! What will become of those who depend on me? Like a blind and mute person whose boat capsizes on a rainy night, cast into a vast sea of cruel waves whose limits cannot be measured, spinning helplessly in a distressful whirlpool with a heart full of deep grief—dying indeed is better, and that is the only fitting end.",
 "modern": "கள் அருந்துவதை விரும்பிய என் தலைவன், கவிழ்ந்த செம்மண் ஈமத்தாழியின் குவிந்த புறத்தே செவிகள் சிவந்த காட்டுக்கோழியும், கூகையும், வலிய வாயையுடைய காகமும், ஆந்தையும் அஞ்சாமல் கூடிப் பேய்க் கூட்டத்தோடு தமக்கு விருப்பமானபடி உலவுகின்ற சுடுகாட்டை அடைந்துவிட்டான். வளையல்களைக் கழற்றிய பெண்களைப் போலப் பாணர்களின் சுற்றத்தார் பழமையான பொலிவிழந்து வருந்துகின்றனர். தோல் போர்த்திய முரசுகளின் கண்கள் கிழிந்து கிடக்கின்றன. காவலர் இல்லாத மலைகளைப் போல யானைகள் தங்கள் தந்தங்களை இழந்துவிட்டன. கொடிய வலிமையுடைய கூற்றுவன் பெரும் துயரத்தை உண்டாக்க, என் தந்தை போன்ற தலைவன் இறந்துபோனதை நான் அறியாமல் போனேனே! அந்தோ, இரங்கத்தக்க நான் இங்கு வந்துவிட்டேனே! என்னை நம்பியிருந்த என் சுற்றத்தார் இனி என்ன ஆவார்களோ? மழைக்காலத்து இரவில் படகு கவிழ்ந்த பொழுதில், மிக்க துன்பமுற்ற நெஞ்சத்தோடு, கண் தெரியாத ஊமை ஒருவன் கடலில் விழுந்தது போல, எல்லையறிய முடியாத அலைகளையுடைய ஆழ்கடலில், துன்பம் தரும் சுழலில் சிக்கித் தவிப்பதைப் போல நான் தவிக்கிறேன். ஆதலால், நான் இறந்துபோவதே நல்லது; அதுவே தகுதியானது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is a poignant lament (kaiyaṟunilai) by the poet Peruñcittiraṉār on the death of his patron, Iḷaveḷimāṉ. The poet arrives at the patron's court only to find that the generous leader has already passed away and reached the cremation ground. He describes the bleakness of the cremation ground where wild birds and ghouls roam freely around the burial urns. The loss of the patron has devastated the bards, whose instruments are broken and whose families are left in deep grief. The poet compares his helpless state to that of a blind and mute person whose boat capsizes in a stormy sea at night, spinning endlessly in a whirlpool. Feeling utterly desolate and worried about his dependents, the poet concludes that death would be a far better and more fitting fate for him.",
